    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "## Problem\n\nThere is no way(?) for an extmark decor to provide \"render one cursor-like cell at this position, whether or not text exists there\".\n\n- `hl_group` recolors an existing glyph, but does not render past EOL (empty lines, EOL insertion points).\n- `virt_text` + `overlay` can fill virtual space, but *replaces* the cell text.\n\nSo anything drawing \"fake cursors\" (multicursor `_core/mcursor.lua:display_win()`, collaborative-editing ghost cursors) must run a decor provider that decides glyph-vs-phantom per mark on every redraw, reading buffer lines to compare the mark column against the line length.\n\n## Expected behavior (unless you know better...)\n\n`hl_group` extmarks can extend past EOL (virtual space). With e.g. `virt=true`, a range whose `end_col` lies past EOL renders highlighted phantom cells up to `end_col`, instead of stopping at EOL. Cell text is decorated in-place, preserving tabs/double-width/composing/conceal.\n\nFor multicursor purposes, this achieves a \"fake cursor\" via `virt=true, end_col=col+1`.\n\nOpen question: multi-line ranges (phantom cells presumably apply per line up to `end_col` on the end line; intermediate lines already have `hl_eol`).\n\nBonus:\n`cursor=true`: the extmark inherits the primary cursor's shape/attributes. This would also let a UI \"promote\" such extmarks to \"terminal cursors\", so they could passively own that decision.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> For multicursor purposes, this achieves a \"fake cursor\" via `virt=true, end_col=col+1`.\n\nIf I understand correctly, displaying something just after EOL can be achieved with `vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns_id, lnum, col+1, { virt_text = { { '│', 'String' } }, virt_text_pos = 'inline' })`. I.e. if the goal is to highlight line's several last cells *and* one cell to the right with the same highlighting, then it can (already) be split into two extmarks. Not ideal, but might work.",
